# SYNOPSIS

```usage
nsd
```

# DESCRIPTION

`nsd` holds the system's namespace groups (ADR-0009).
A group is a namespace that several processes share, as a Plan 9 `Pgrp` is: a `bind` or `mount` one member makes is seen by every other.
svcd(8) starts it from `boot/svc/nsd.ndb` and posts it as `/srv/nsd`.
It is not restarted: the groups live only in it.

`nsd` serves no files.
Its post, and each member's channel, take the calls of `lib/vx-ns/nsd.h`, which vx-ns makes for every process; a process reaches the post through the connector `srv:nsd` its spawn message carries, which a manifest's `connect=nsd` gives a service (see svc(6)).

A group is three things:

- its namespace, as namespace(6) text, the lines ns(1) prints, at most 16 KiB;
- the connectors its `mount` lines name, at most 8, each under the source name it is mounted as (`bootfs`, `proc`), of fewer than 64 bytes;
- a VMO holding a sequence number and the text, which `nsd` writes and each member maps read-only.

The sequence is odd while `nsd` writes the text and even after, so a member reads the text whole.
Each member keeps a mount table of its own, made from the text: before it resolves a name it looks at the sequence, and if it has moved it empties its table and replays the text.
So resolving a name takes no call to `nsd`, and a member opens its own connection to each server, through a duplicate of the group's connector.

A member changes the namespace by binding, mounting or unmounting in its own table, then sending `nsd` the text it now prints with the sequence it started from.
`nsd` takes the text only if no other member changed the group meanwhile; otherwise the member catches up and tries again.

vx-ns makes a group the first time a process spawns a child that shares its namespace, with the process as the first member, and gives the child a member channel of its own (`nsgroup` in its spawn message).
A process that never shares never talks to `nsd`, and without `nsd` a child gets a copy.
A fork's child gets a channel of its own and maps the text again.
svcd gives each service a namespace of its own, not a group.

Each member has its own channel, and the channel says which group it is in.
`nsd` waits on one port for its post and every member channel; a member whose channel closes leaves its group, and a group goes when its last member does.
It holds 32 groups and 128 members in all.

@node=calls title="The calls"

A call is a channel message: a `vx_msg_header`, whose ordinal names the call, then the arguments, then bytes:

```text
typedef struct nsd_args {
  uint64_t seq;       // UPDATE: the sequence the text was made from;
                      // a reply: the group's sequence now
  uint64_t task;      // NEW, HELLO: the caller's task id;
                      // TEXT: the one asked about
  uint32_t text_len;  // the bytes after nsd_args that are text
  uint32_t count;     // NEW, UPDATE: connectors given
} nsd_args;
```

The reply has the same shape; its header's flags are 0 or a status.
Connectors travel as the message's handles, each named in order by a line, the source's name and a newline, after the text.
On `/srv/nsd`:

: `NSD_NEW`
  A new group, from the namespace's text and its connectors.
  The reply carries the caller's member channel and a read-only handle to the group's VMO.
  `INVALID` if the text is longer than 16 KiB or longer than the message, the count does not match the handles, or a name is empty, too long or there is no room for the connectors; `NO_MEMORY` if 32 groups exist or no member slot is free.
: `NSD_TEXT`
  The text of the group task `task` is in, after the arguments in the reply: what procfs(4) serves as `/proc/<pid>/ns`.
  `NOT_FOUND` if no member said it was that task.

On a member's channel:

: `NSD_SHARE`
  A member channel for another member of the same group, to give a child; the reply carries it.
: `NSD_HELLO`
  A new member saying which task it is; the reply carries a read-only handle to the group's VMO.
: `NSD_UPDATE`
  The group's new text, made from sequence `seq`, and any connectors new to it.
  `BAD_STATE` (stale) if the group's sequence is no longer `seq`; `INVALID` for a text too long or a count that does not match; `NO_MEMORY` if the connectors cannot be taken.
: `NSD_CONNECTOR`
  A duplicate of the connector for the source named by the text; `NOT_FOUND` if the group has none.

Any other ordinal is answered with `INVALID`.
The connectors a call carries are taken all or none: a source the group has already keeps its connector, and the new handle is closed; when they cannot all be taken, every handle sent is closed.

# EXAMPLES

The manifest that starts it, `boot/svc/nsd.ndb`:

```ndb
service=nsd program=/boot/bin/nsd post=nsd console
```

The console shell's manifest, `boot/svc/shell.ndb`, ends with `connect=nsd`, so the commands it runs share its namespace group:

```ndb
service=rc program=/boot/bin/rc console restart
mount=/ srv=bootfs
bind=/bin new=/boot/bin flags=a
mount=/proc srv=proc
mount=/net srv=net
mount=/sys srv=sys
mount=/tmp srv=tmpfs
connect=nsd
```

# FILES

: `/srv/nsd`
  nsd's post.
: `/proc/<pid>/ns`
  A process's group's text, through procfs(4).

